<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wadi Hadhramaut</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wadi Hadhramaut is a long valley system in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, running roughly parallel to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> coast. Watered by seasonal floods, its fields supported the ancient Kingdom of Hadramawt, and in later centuries towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibam">Shibam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seiyun">Seiyun</a> and Tarim grew there, famous for their mud-brick tower houses. The old walled city of Shibam is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wadi-hadhramaut.jpg" alt="Wadi Hadhramaut" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jialiang Gao www.peace-on-earth.org [dead link] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shibam_Wadi_Hadhramaut_Yemen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
